📄 User.java (Entity)
1. Purpose
Represents the users table in the database.
Maps a Java object to a database table using JPA/Hibernate.
Each object of this class represents one row in the table.
2. Annotations Used
@Entity

Purpose

Marks this class as a JPA entity.
Tells Hibernate to map this class to a database table.
@Table(name = "users")

Purpose

Specifies the database table name.
Optional. If omitted, Hibernate uses the class name.
@Id

Purpose

Marks the Primary Key.
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)

Purpose

Database generates the primary key automatically using AUTO_INCREMENT.
Lombok

@Getter

Generates getters.

@Setter

Generates setters.

@NoArgsConstructor

Generates a no-argument constructor.
Required by Hibernate (uses reflection to create entity objects).

@AllArgsConstructor

Generates a constructor with all fields.
3. Interview Questions
Q1. What is an Entity?

Answer

An Entity is a Java class that represents a database table. Each object of the entity represents one row in the table.

Q2. Why do we use @Entity?

To tell JPA/Hibernate that this class should be mapped to a database table.

Q3. Is @Table mandatory?

No. If not provided, Hibernate uses the class name as the table name.

Q4. Why do we use @Id?

It identifies the primary key of the table.

Q5. Why GenerationType.IDENTITY?

It lets the database generate the primary key automatically using an auto-increment column.

Q6. Why is @NoArgsConstructor required?

Hibernate creates entity objects using reflection, so it requires a no-argument constructor.

Q7. Why use Long instead of long?

Long is an object and can be null. JPA works better with wrapper classes because they can represent an unsaved entity whose ID hasn't been generated yet.

Q8. What is Hibernate?

Hibernate is the most commonly used JPA implementation. It converts Java objects into SQL queries and manages the mapping between objects and database tables.

